1
00:00:00,660 --> 00:00:01,820
April fifth.

2
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,140
Rent day.

3
00:00:03,540 --> 00:00:05,780
Meera counts out five thousand rupees and

4
00:00:05,860 --> 00:00:07,140
hands it to the landlord.

5
00:00:07,709 --> 00:00:10,349
Last time, every rupee that left the galla

6
00:00:10,509 --> 00:00:13,389
came back as something — a cart, some tea

7
00:00:13,469 --> 00:00:14,029
and sugar.

8
00:00:14,509 --> 00:00:15,509
But this time?

9
00:00:16,389 --> 00:00:17,989
No new asset arrives.

10
00:00:18,069 --> 00:00:19,589
No debt gets smaller.

11
00:00:19,669 --> 00:00:20,869
Nothing comes back.

12
00:00:22,155 --> 00:00:23,502
The scale tips.

13
00:00:23,782 --> 00:00:25,782
So what's on the other side?

14
00:00:31,991 --> 00:00:34,151
First, last time's answers.

15
00:00:34,471 --> 00:00:35,071
One.

16
00:00:35,351 --> 00:00:38,111
After Gopal's delivery, the equation reads:

17
00:00:38,311 --> 00:00:40,151
eighty-eight thousand equals thirty-eight

18
00:00:40,311 --> 00:00:42,631
thousand, plus fifty thousand.

19
00:00:43,311 --> 00:00:43,831
Two.

20
00:00:44,111 --> 00:00:46,111
Buying the cart for cash doesn't move the

21
00:00:46,191 --> 00:00:46,911
scale.

22
00:00:47,071 --> 00:00:49,631
Cash goes down, the cart comes in — a swap

23
00:00:49,671 --> 00:00:50,751
on the left.

24
00:00:51,431 --> 00:00:51,991
Three.

25
00:00:52,231 --> 00:00:54,471
Paying back part of Ravi Mama's loan?

26
00:00:55,311 --> 00:00:58,271
Cash goes down, and so does what Meera owes.

27
00:00:58,391 --> 00:01:00,431
Both sides go down together, and the scale

28
00:01:00,511 --> 00:01:01,551
stays level.

29
00:01:02,131 --> 00:01:04,131
And paying back a loan is not an expense

30
00:01:04,451 --> 00:01:06,131
— the debt just got smaller.

31
00:01:07,179 --> 00:01:08,699
So, back to rent day.

32
00:01:09,179 --> 00:01:10,019
Freeze.

33
00:01:10,299 --> 00:01:11,899
Which two things changed?

34
00:01:13,855 --> 00:01:15,455
The first one is easy.

35
00:01:15,615 --> 00:01:17,695
Cash went down by five thousand.

36
00:01:17,775 --> 00:01:18,895
But the second?

37
00:01:19,575 --> 00:01:22,215
Ravi Mama is still owed thirty thousand.

38
00:01:22,255 --> 00:01:24,695
Gopal is still owed eight thousand.

39
00:01:24,895 --> 00:01:26,615
Nobody's debt got smaller.

40
00:01:27,255 --> 00:01:29,455
So the change has to land on the only thing

41
00:01:29,535 --> 00:01:31,855
left — Meera's own share.

42
00:01:31,975 --> 00:01:32,655
Equity.

43
00:01:33,189 --> 00:01:35,109
Let's unfold Meera's tag.

44
00:01:35,349 --> 00:01:38,389
It opens into a card — the equity card —

45
00:01:38,469 --> 00:01:39,669
with two pockets.

46
00:01:40,409 --> 00:01:42,969
Capital: the fifty thousand Meera put in.

47
00:01:43,049 --> 00:01:44,969
And a new, empty pocket.

48
00:01:45,509 --> 00:01:47,629
The rent comes out of that new pocket.

49
00:01:47,709 --> 00:01:50,509
For now, it reads minus five thousand.

50
00:01:51,062 --> 00:01:53,382
Eighty-three thousand equals thirty-eight

51
00:01:53,542 --> 00:01:56,422
thousand, plus forty-five thousand.

52
00:01:56,462 --> 00:01:57,622
The scale settles.

53
00:01:58,702 --> 00:02:00,942
Money that is used up to run the business,

54
00:02:01,022 --> 00:02:03,742
gone for good, is called an expense.

55
00:02:04,582 --> 00:02:07,142
An expense shrinks the owner's share.

56
00:02:08,559 --> 00:02:09,959
Now the fun part.

57
00:02:10,159 --> 00:02:11,959
Meera opens for business.

58
00:02:12,159 --> 00:02:14,479
Raju, her new helper, keeps the glasses

59
00:02:14,559 --> 00:02:15,119
moving.

60
00:02:15,359 --> 00:02:18,559
Two weeks of chai — glass after glass after

61
00:02:18,719 --> 00:02:19,439
glass.

62
00:02:19,797 --> 00:02:22,517
By April fifteenth, the galla has collected

63
00:02:22,597 --> 00:02:24,997
eighteen thousand rupees from sales.

64
00:02:25,157 --> 00:02:27,397
Cash goes up by eighteen thousand.

65
00:02:27,477 --> 00:02:28,837
Meera, your turn.

66
00:02:28,917 --> 00:02:30,197
Where's the second change?

67
00:02:32,246 --> 00:02:34,406
She didn't borrow it, so there's no new

68
00:02:34,486 --> 00:02:35,206
debt.

69
00:02:35,286 --> 00:02:38,006
She didn't add her savings, so capital stays

70
00:02:38,086 --> 00:02:39,366
at fifty thousand.

71
00:02:39,526 --> 00:02:40,566
She earned it.

72
00:02:40,686 --> 00:02:43,126
So it goes into the new pocket — right beside

73
00:02:43,206 --> 00:02:43,926
the rent.

74
00:02:44,396 --> 00:02:47,116
One lakh one thousand equals thirty-eight

75
00:02:47,276 --> 00:02:50,156
thousand, plus sixty-three thousand.

76
00:02:50,876 --> 00:02:52,676
Money the business earns by selling what

77
00:02:52,756 --> 00:02:55,076
it sells is called revenue.

78
00:02:55,756 --> 00:02:58,076
Revenue grows the owner's share.

79
00:02:59,331 --> 00:03:01,571
Now peek inside that new pocket.

80
00:03:01,811 --> 00:03:04,051
Eighteen thousand came in as revenue.

81
00:03:04,131 --> 00:03:06,531
Five thousand went out as an expense.

82
00:03:06,611 --> 00:03:07,491
What's left?

83
00:03:09,255 --> 00:03:10,855
Thirteen thousand rupees.

84
00:03:11,055 --> 00:03:15,175
That's profit, so far — revenue, minus expenses.

85
00:03:16,115 --> 00:03:17,795
Now the pocket has a name.

86
00:03:18,655 --> 00:03:20,095
And look where it lives.

87
00:03:20,295 --> 00:03:23,335
Inside equity, right next to Meera's capital.

88
00:03:24,075 --> 00:03:26,755
Ravi Mama and Gopal are owed fixed amounts.

89
00:03:26,955 --> 00:03:29,275
But every rupee of profit belongs to the

90
00:03:29,355 --> 00:03:29,995
owner.

91
00:03:30,546 --> 00:03:33,066
So our equation gets a little longer.

92
00:03:33,346 --> 00:03:36,306
Assets equal liabilities, plus capital,

93
00:03:36,426 --> 00:03:38,626
plus revenue, minus expenses.

94
00:03:39,626 --> 00:03:40,346
Same scale.

95
00:03:40,506 --> 00:03:42,666
We just looked inside Meera's share.

96
00:03:43,913 --> 00:03:46,553
Now, here's the trap most new owners fall

97
00:03:46,713 --> 00:03:46,953
into.

98
00:03:47,513 --> 00:03:50,873
Money left the galla, so it must be an expense.

99
00:03:51,396 --> 00:03:51,916
Nope.

100
00:03:52,116 --> 00:03:53,156
Remember the cart?

101
00:03:53,996 --> 00:03:55,836
Thirty-six thousand rupees left the galla

102
00:03:55,916 --> 00:03:56,876
for that too.

103
00:03:56,956 --> 00:03:58,476
So ask one question.

104
00:03:59,116 --> 00:04:01,196
Is it used up — gone for good?

105
00:04:02,036 --> 00:04:02,756
The rent?

106
00:04:03,596 --> 00:04:04,116
Yes.

107
00:04:04,316 --> 00:04:07,036
April is paid for, and May will need more.

108
00:04:07,116 --> 00:04:07,836
The cart?

109
00:04:08,676 --> 00:04:09,236
No.

110
00:04:09,276 --> 00:04:11,156
It's still standing right there, making

111
00:04:11,236 --> 00:04:11,956
chai.

112
00:04:12,576 --> 00:04:13,736
That's an asset.

113
00:04:14,984 --> 00:04:15,824
Your turn.

114
00:04:16,104 --> 00:04:18,344
Just imagine this — it's not in Meera's

115
00:04:18,424 --> 00:04:19,024
books.

116
00:04:19,304 --> 00:04:21,464
Meera pays three hundred rupees for a gas

117
00:04:21,544 --> 00:04:22,224
refill.

118
00:04:22,504 --> 00:04:24,264
Which two things changed?

119
00:04:27,431 --> 00:04:29,751
Cash, down three hundred.

120
00:04:29,791 --> 00:04:32,471
And the profit pocket, down three hundred.

121
00:04:32,951 --> 00:04:35,591
The gas burns away making chai — it's used

122
00:04:35,671 --> 00:04:35,911
up.

123
00:04:36,231 --> 00:04:37,191
An expense.

124
00:04:37,757 --> 00:04:38,597
One more.

125
00:04:38,877 --> 00:04:41,517
Meera buys a second kettle for fifteen hundred

126
00:04:41,597 --> 00:04:42,357
rupees.

127
00:04:42,637 --> 00:04:44,317
Which two things changed?

128
00:04:47,487 --> 00:04:49,887
Cash, down fifteen hundred.

129
00:04:49,927 --> 00:04:52,607
And Equipment, up fifteen hundred — a swap

130
00:04:52,647 --> 00:04:53,567
on the left.

131
00:04:54,047 --> 00:04:55,807
The profit pocket doesn't move.

132
00:04:55,847 --> 00:04:58,127
The kettle stays, and keeps making chai

133
00:04:58,207 --> 00:04:59,327
for years.

134
00:04:59,407 --> 00:05:00,367
An asset.

135
00:05:01,622 --> 00:05:04,382
So — Meera's share has two pockets.

136
00:05:04,582 --> 00:05:06,622
Revenue fills the profit pocket.

137
00:05:06,822 --> 00:05:08,502
Expenses drain it.

138
00:05:08,542 --> 00:05:10,342
And profit is the difference.

139
00:05:11,474 --> 00:05:12,194
Your turn.

140
00:05:12,354 --> 00:05:13,354
Three quick ones.

141
00:05:13,554 --> 00:05:14,034
One.

142
00:05:14,274 --> 00:05:16,274
Is the thirty-six-thousand-rupee cart an

143
00:05:16,354 --> 00:05:17,194
expense?

144
00:05:19,274 --> 00:05:19,914
Two.

145
00:05:19,994 --> 00:05:22,154
What's Meera's profit after the rent and

146
00:05:22,234 --> 00:05:23,994
the first two weeks of sales?

147
00:05:25,874 --> 00:05:26,594
Three.

148
00:05:26,674 --> 00:05:28,594
Which side of the scale does profit live

149
00:05:28,674 --> 00:05:29,314
on?

150
00:05:31,354 --> 00:05:33,274
Answers at the start of the next lesson.

151
00:05:34,685 --> 00:05:37,085
Next time, the office next door takes chai

152
00:05:37,165 --> 00:05:39,885
for the whole floor, every single day.

153
00:05:40,125 --> 00:05:41,125
Just one catch.

154
00:05:41,325 --> 00:05:42,365
They pay later.
